Golden Root (ADR-0017) against real S3 on real hardware.

host:     AWS i4i.metal, 128 logical cpus, 1 TiB RAM, Ubuntu 24.04, kernel 6.17
region:   us-east-1, S3 bucket in-region, temporary
build:    release, this branch
date_utc: 2026-07-27

blockd/tests/golden_root_cost.rs with CATS_BENCH_S3_BUCKET set, so every object is
a real PUT round trip instead of a local create + sync_data + hard_link.

  copy      = stream the image into the volume and commit it (what a create did)
  reference = publish the image once, provision by referencing its chunks

One create:

 image MiB       path uploaded MiB    objects provision ms    speedup        gen
        64       copy        64.00         19       1977.6       1.0x          1
        64  reference         0.00          3        897.4       2.2x          1
        64  (publish)         once          -       1601.8          -          -
       256       copy       256.01         67       7534.7       1.0x          1
       256  reference         0.01          3         88.1      85.5x          1
       256  (publish)         once          -       6719.0          -          -
       512       copy       512.01        131      15472.6       1.0x          1
       512  reference         0.02          3         89.7     172.6x          1
       512  (publish)         once          -       8310.1          -          -

The 64 MiB "reference" row at 897 ms is the first S3 call of the process and
carries connection and TLS setup; the steady-state figure is the ~88-90 ms of the
256 and 512 MiB rows, and it does not grow with the image.

N Cats from one image at once (256 MiB image, lease TTL raised, see below):

    cats       path uploaded MiB    objects        wall ms    speedup
       1       copy       256.01         67         7796.7       1.0x
       1  reference         0.01          3          152.7      51.0x
       4       copy      1024.02        268        13827.6       1.0x
       4  reference         0.04         12          208.0      66.5x
      10       copy      2560.05        670        27230.0       1.0x
      10  reference         0.11         30          167.4     162.6x

Reading:

* On S3 the win is larger than on a local store (172x vs 178x for one create is a
  coincidence of different baselines; for 10 concurrent it is 162x on S3 against
  102x locally) because the copy side pays a PUT round trip per 4 MiB object.
* The reference side is flat in both the image size and the number of Cats:
  ~90-210 ms regardless. It writes map nodes and a commit record, nothing else.
* Publishing 512 MiB costs 8.3 s once per image digest.

Two things this run found, both fixed on this branch:

1. The first attempt at wiring this had `cats-blockd --seed-image` publish the
   image *after* attaching, i.e. while holding the volume lease. The node agent
   configures a 1.5 s lease TTL, and an 8 s upload lost it:

     INFO  attached volume=vol_f11ad38fc6d3 epoch=1 generation=0
     ERROR lease renewal failed; fencing writes: lease epoch is stale or expired
     Error: seed volume from image /opt/cats/guest/rootfs.ext4
            provision from base image: attachment is fenced; refusing writes

   Publishing is fleet-wide work and needs no lease, so it now happens before the
   attach, and the node agent publishes once per node via
   `cats-blockd --publish-image` and passes `--seed-image-id` to each launch.
   A launch therefore pays one manifest GET plus a map write.

2. The concurrent case of the benchmark itself failed the same way
   (CasConflict{expected: 0}) because a 27 s copy-path commit outlives the
   5 s default lease. Benchmarks that deliberately run multi-second commits now
   set CATS_BENCH_LEASE_TTL_MS. Worth noting as an operational property: with
   full-chunk commits a 512 MiB seed commit takes 15 s on S3 against a 1.5 s
   lease, and survives only because renewal runs concurrently. Delta packs
   shorten commits by the same factor they shrink them.
